==Career-based timeline==
This timeline shows most of the early life, the political career and death of each prime minister of the United Kingdom from 1846.  The first prime minister was [[Robert Walpole]] in the early 18th century {{harv|Dickinson|1973}}.

Unlike countries where the leader is elected directly to the highest political office of a separate [[executive (government)|executive]], the [[Prime Minister of the United Kingdom|prime minister]] must first establish a political career in the UK Parliament and typically serves many years in the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] before becoming prime minister, and in some cases for many years afterwards.

Since the [[Robert Gascoyne-Cecil, 3rd Marquess of Salisbury|Marquess of Salisbury]] in 1895, all time in parliamentary service before being prime minister has been in the House of Commons, apart from Sir [[Alec Douglas-Home]]'s period as a member of the Government while in the House of Lords (1951–63; though he was previously the elected member for [[Lanark (UK Parliament constituency)|Lanark]], 1931–1945).  After becoming prime minister, Douglas-Home returned to the Commons by winning a by-election on the recess death of MP [[Gilmour Leburn]].

Uniquely, [[David Cameron|Cameron]]'s brief parliamentary activity as [[Foreign Secretary (United Kingdom)|Foreign Secretary]] from November 2023 to July 2024 was served while a member of the [[House of Lords]].

===Key===
* Each dark coloured bar denotes the time spent as prime minister
* A light colour denotes time spent in Parliament before or after serving as prime minister
* A grey colour bar denotes the time the prime minister spent outside the House of Commons or the entire Parliament, either before or after their political career

===Notable moments since 1800===
* ''two separate periods in office:'' [[John Russell, 1st Earl Russell|Russell]], [[Henry John Temple, 3rd Viscount Palmerston|Palmerston]], [[Benjamin Disraeli|Disraeli]], [[Ramsay MacDonald|MacDonald]], [[Winston Churchill|Churchill]], [[Harold Wilson|Wilson]]
* ''three separate periods in office:'' [[Edward Smith-Stanley, 14th Earl of Derby|Derby]], [[Robert Gascoyne-Cecil, 3rd Marquess of Salisbury|Salisbury]], [[Stanley Baldwin|Baldwin]]
* ''four separate periods in office:'' [[William Ewart Gladstone|Gladstone]]
* ''[[Crossing the floor|crossed the floor]]:'' [[Henry John Temple, 3rd Viscount Palmerston|Palmerston]], [[Edward Smith-Stanley, 14th Earl of Derby|Derby]], [[William Ewart Gladstone|Gladstone]], [[Winston Churchill|Churchill]] (twice)
* ''died in office:'' [[George Canning|Canning]], [[Henry John Temple, 3rd Viscount Palmerston|Palmerston]], [[Spencer Perceval|Perceval]] ([[Assassination of Spencer Perceval|assassinated]])
* ''died less than six months after leaving office:'' [[William Cavendish-Bentinck, 3rd Duke of Portland|Portland]], [[Henry Campbell-Bannerman|Campbell-Bannerman]], [[Bonar Law|Law]], [[Neville Chamberlain|Chamberlain]]
* ''left Parliament on leaving office:'' [[John Russell, 1st Earl Russell|Russell]], [[Stanley Baldwin|Baldwin]], [[Tony Blair|Blair]]
* ''lived for more than twenty years after leaving the House of Commons:'' [[Harold Macmillan|Macmillan]], [[Alec Douglas-Home|Douglas-Home]], [[Margaret Thatcher|Thatcher]], [[John Major|Major]]
* ''less than twenty years in Parliament before being prime minister:'' [[Stanley Baldwin|Baldwin]], [[Neville Chamberlain|Chamberlain]], [[Harold Wilson|Wilson]], [[John Major|Major]], [[Tony Blair|Blair]], [[David Cameron|Cameron]], [[Theresa May|May]], [[Boris Johnson|Johnson]], [[Liz Truss|Truss]], [[Rishi Sunak|Sunak]], [[Keir Starmer|Starmer]]
* ''more than twenty years in Parliament after being prime minister:'' [[Archibald Primrose, 5th Earl of Rosebery|Rosebery]], [[Arthur Balfour|Balfour]], [[David Lloyd George|Lloyd George]], [[Edward Heath|Heath]]
* ''served as prime minister after an interruption to their parliamentary career:'' [[Ramsay MacDonald|MacDonald]], [[Winston Churchill|Churchill]], [[Harold Macmillan|Macmillan]] (twice), [[Alec Douglas-Home|Douglas-Home]], [[Boris Johnson|Johnson]] <!--Boris Johnson had an interruption to his parliamentary career, but not his political career, as he served as the elected Mayor of London during the entire interruption-->
* ''served as prime minister for two monarchs:'' [[Robert Walpole|Walpole]], [[Thomas Pelham-Holles, 1st Duke of Newcastle|Newcastle]], [[Robert Jenkinson, 2nd Earl of Liverpool|Liverpool]], [[Arthur Wellesley, 1st Duke of Wellington|Wellington]], [[William Lamb, 2nd Viscount Melbourne|Melbourne]], [[Robert Peel|Peel]], [[Robert Gascoyne-Cecil, 3rd Marquess of Salisbury|Salisbury]], [[H. H. Asquith|Asquith]], [[Winston Churchill|Churchill]], [[Liz Truss|Truss]]
* ''served as prime minister for three monarchs:'' [[Stanley Baldwin|Baldwin]]
* ''served as prime minister for less than two months:'' [[Liz Truss|Truss]]
* ''over 65 years of age when first appointed:'' [[Henry John Temple, 3rd Viscount Palmerston|Palmerston]], [[Henry Campbell-Bannerman|Campbell-Bannerman]], [[George Hamilton-Gordon, 4th Earl of Aberdeen|Aberdeen]], [[Neville Chamberlain|Chamberlain]], [[Charles Grey, 2nd Earl Grey|Grey]], [[Winston Churchill|Churchill]]
* ''under 45 years of age when first appointed:'' [[F. J. Robinson, 1st Viscount Goderich|Goderich]], [[Tony Blair|Blair]], [[Henry Addington|Addington]], [[David Cameron|Cameron]], [[Rishi Sunak|Sunak]], [[Robert Jenkinson, 2nd Earl of Liverpool|Liverpool]]

